[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Collection Expressions in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Collection expressions provide a concise and readable syntax for creating common collection values like arrays, spans, and lists. They simplify collection initialization, reducing boilerplate code and improving code clarity."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Arrays"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Collection expressions can be used to initialize arrays directly. Instead of using the 'new' keyword and specifying the array size, you can simply enclose the elements within square brackets."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"int[] numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
string[] names = ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"];"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"This is equivalent to the traditional array initialization:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"int[] numbers = new int[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
string[] names = new string[] { "Alice", "Bob", "Charlie" };"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Spans"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Collection expressions can also be used to create 'Span<T>' instances. Spans provide a way to work with contiguous regions of memory without copying data."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"Span<int> numbersSpan = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Lists"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Initializing lists with collection expressions is straightforward. The elements are enclosed in square brackets, and the compiler infers the list type."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"List<string> colors = ["Red", "Green", "Blue"];"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"This is a more concise alternative to:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"List<string> colors = new List<string> { "Red", "Green", "Blue" };"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Spread Operator"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Collection expressions support the spread operator ('..'), which allows you to include the elements of an existing collection into a new collection."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"int[] firstHalf = [1, 2, 3];
int[] secondHalf = [4, 5, 6];
int[] combined = [..firstHalf, ..secondHalf]; // combined will be [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The spread operator simplifies combining multiple collections into one."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Null Handling"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"If a spread element is null, the collection expression will throw an 'ArgumentNullException'. Be sure to handle null collections appropriately before using the spread operator."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"int[]? nullableArray = null;
// int[] combined = [..nullableArray]; // This will throw an exception."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Custom Collection Types"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Collection expressions can be used with custom collection types if they implement the required interfaces or have appropriate 'Create' methods. The compiler uses pattern matching to determine how to construct the collection."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The type must have a 'Create' method that accepts a 'ReadOnlySpan<T>'."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The type must implement 'System.Collections.IEnumerable' or 'System.Collections.Generic.IEnumerable<T>'."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Collection Expressions"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved readability: Collection expressions provide a more concise and intuitive syntax for collection initialization."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced boilerplate: They eliminate the need for explicit 'new' keyword and type specifications in many cases."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enhanced code maintainability: The simplified syntax makes code easier to understand and maintain."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Collection expressions are a powerful feature in C# that simplifies collection initialization and improves code clarity. By using collection expressions, you can write more concise and readable code when working with arrays, spans, lists, and other collection types."}]
